Step 1. The corollary, as pure group theory.
========================================================================
  N= 3: N-cycle [3]   its square [3]   compatible
  N= 4: N-cycle [4]   its square [2, 2]   INCOMPATIBLE -> relation false
  N= 5: N-cycle [5]   its square [5]   compatible
  N= 6: N-cycle [6]   its square [3, 3]   INCOMPATIBLE -> relation false
  N= 7: N-cycle [7]   its square [7]   compatible
  N= 8: N-cycle [8]   its square [4, 4]   INCOMPATIBLE -> relation false
  N= 9: N-cycle [9]   its square [9]   compatible
  N=10: N-cycle [10]   its square [5, 5]   INCOMPATIBLE -> relation false

========================================================================
Step 2. Check the proposition on the real orbit (N=3).
========================================================================
  vineyard   permutation cycle lengths [3]   exponent sum +0
  trajectory permutation cycle lengths [3]   exponent sum +0
  both are 3-cycles, as the proposition requires: True
  square of trajectory permutation: [3]
  permutation test at N=3: [3] vs [3] -> passes, and passes automatically
  exponent test at N=3: e(V)=+0 vs 2e(T)=+0 -> passes, and both sides are zero

========================================================================
VERDICT
========================================================================
  The relation is FALSE for every even N, by the corollary.
  At N=3, the only case where it was ever observed, both available
  conjugation invariants are incapable of detecting a difference.
  So the claim is refuted in general and was never supported at N=3.
  No statement is made for odd N >= 5, and none is needed: a relation
  that fails for every even N is not a relation.
